VB2=[
X(29,'LIKE MOTHS TO A FLAME',
 S('Moths flying into a flame','','lamp','PATAṄGĀḤ',NIGHT2,''.join(f'<path d="M{130+k*14} {30+k*12} q6 -6 12 0" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(5))),
 '“As moths rush with great speed into a blazing fire to their destruction, so these people rush with great speed into your mouths to their destruction.”',
 [('yathā pradīptam jvalanam pataṅgāḥ','<b>as moths into a blazing fire</b>'),('viśanti nāśāya samṛddha-vegāḥ','<b>enter to perish, at full speed</b>'),('tathā eva nāśāya viśanti lokāḥ','<b>so these people enter to perish</b>'),('tava api vaktrāṇi samṛddha-vegāḥ','<b>your mouths, at full speed</b>')],
 'A second image: moths drawn to a flame.',
 'Rivers flow naturally; moths are drawn by attraction to what destroys them. Commentators read this as beings drawn by their own desires and karma toward their end. The Kauravas’ greed draws them to the war.',
 'What attracts us is not always good for us. Watch the flames you fly toward.',
 'Like moths <b>to the flame</b>.'),
X(30,'DEVOURING THE WORLDS',
 CS('Flaming tongues licking around the worlds, rays scorching everything','DEVOURING'),
 '“Devouring all the worlds on every side with your flaming mouths, you lick your lips. Your fierce rays, filling the whole universe with brilliance, scorch it, Viṣṇu.”',
 [('lelihyase grasamānaḥ samantāt','<b>you lick, devouring on all sides</b>'),('lokān samagrān vadanaiḥ jvaladbhiḥ','<b>all the worlds with flaming mouths</b>'),('tejobhiḥ āpūrya jagat samagram','<b>filling the whole world with light</b>'),('bhāsaḥ tava ugrāḥ pratapanti viṣṇo','<b>your fierce rays scorch, Viṣṇu</b>')],
 'Arjuna describes the devouring aspect of the Lord.',
 'Commentators of all schools see Time here: everything in the world is consumed in its turn. The image is meant to stun Arjuna out of his small view of the war.',
 'Time consumes all things. Nothing we build is permanent; only what we are is.',
 'Time <b>devours all</b>.'),
X(31,'“WHO ARE YOU?”',
 S('Arjuna bowing and asking the fierce form who it is','Af','cosmic','WHO ARE YOU?'),
 '“Tell me who you are, so fierce in form. Salutations to you, best of gods; be gracious. I wish to know you, the primal one, for I do not understand your purpose.”',
 [('ākhyāhi me kaḥ bhavān ugra-rūpaḥ','<b>tell me who you are, fierce in form</b>'),('namaḥ astu te deva-vara prasīda','<b>salutations, best of gods, be gracious</b>'),('vijñātum icchāmi bhavantam ādyam','<b>I wish to know you, the primal one</b>'),('na hi prajānāmi tava pravṛttim','<b>I do not understand your purpose</b>')],
 'Arjuna asks: who are you, and what are you doing?',
 'Even in terror Arjuna bows and asks. Commentators praise the combination: reverence and honest questioning. He knows the form is Kṛṣṇa, yet asks what this aspect means.',
 'When life shows its fierce face, ask what it is teaching.',
 '<b>Who are you</b>, so fierce?'),
X(32,'“I AM TIME”',
 CS('The cosmic form declaring itself as Time, destroyer of worlds','KĀLO’SMI'),
 'The Lord said: I am Time, grown mighty, the destroyer of worlds, now engaged in destroying these people. Even without you, none of these warriors arrayed in the opposing armies will live.',
 [('kālaḥ asmi','<b>I am Time</b>'),('loka-kṣaya-kṛt pravṛddhaḥ','<b>grown mighty, destroyer of worlds</b>'),('lokān samāhartum iha pravṛttaḥ','<b>here engaged in destroying the people</b>'),('ṛte api tvām na bhaviṣyanti sarve','<b>even without you, none will survive</b>'),('ye avasthitāḥ pratyanīkeṣu yodhāḥ','<b>the warriors in the opposing ranks</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa answers: I am Time.',
 'Oppenheimer quoted a version of this verse, “Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds”, after the first atomic test. In the Gītā the point is different: the war’s outcome is already decided by Time; Arjuna’s refusal would change nothing except his own part in it.',
 'Many things will happen with or without us. The question is what part we play.',
 '<b>“I am Time</b>, destroyer of worlds.”'),
X(33,'BE ONLY THE INSTRUMENT',
 S('Arjuna drawing his bow, guided by Krishna’s hand','KA','bow','NIMITTA-MĀTRAM'),
 '“Therefore stand up and win glory! Conquer your enemies and enjoy a prosperous kingdom. They have already been slain by me; be merely the instrument, Savyasācin.”',
 [('tasmāt tvam uttiṣṭha yaśaḥ labhasva','<b>so stand up, win glory</b>'),('jitvā śatrūn bhuṅkṣva rājyam samṛddham','<b>conquer foes, enjoy a rich kingdom</b>'),('mayā eva ete nihatāḥ pūrvam eva','<b>by me they are already slain</b>'),('nimitta-mātram bhava savya-sācin','<b>be merely the instrument, ambidextrous archer</b>')],
 'The key verse of the chapter: be only my instrument.',
 'Arjuna feared being the killer (1.31: <i>nimitta</i>, omens). Now Kṛṣṇa uses the same word: be <i>nimitta-mātra</i>, only an instrument. <i>Savyasācin</i>: one who can shoot with either hand. Commentators: the burden of doership is lifted; Arjuna acts as God’s hand.',
 'Do your part as an instrument of the good, not as the owner of outcomes.',
 'Be <b>only the instrument</b>.'),
X(34,'ALREADY SLAIN BY ME',
 S('Krishna pointing to Drona, Bhishma, Jayadratha and Karna, already fading','K','army','ALREADY SLAIN'),
 '“Droṇa and Bhīṣma, Jayadratha and Karṇa, and the other heroic warriors, already slain by me, you must slay. Do not be distressed. Fight! You will conquer your enemies in battle.”',
 [('droṇam ca bhīṣmam ca jayadratham ca','<b>Droṇa, Bhīṣma, Jayadratha</b>'),('karṇam tathā anyān api yodha-vīrān','<b>Karṇa and other heroes</b>'),('mayā hatān tvam jahi mā vyathiṣṭhāḥ','<b>slain by me, kill them; do not grieve</b>'),('yudhyasva jetā asi raṇe sapatnān','<b>fight; you will win in battle</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa names the great warriors Arjuna fears to face.',
 'Each named hero was thought unbeatable: Droṇa the teacher, Bhīṣma with a boon over his death, Jayadratha protected by his father’s curse, Karṇa with divine weapons. Kṛṣṇa says: their end is already decided.',
 'Obstacles that look unbeatable may already be weaker than they seem.',
 'Already slain: <b>do not grieve</b>.'),
X(35,'ARJUNA SPEAKS, TREMBLING',
 S('Sanjaya describing Arjuna bowing again and again, voice faltering','S','cosmic','TREMBLING'),
 'Sañjaya said: Hearing these words of Keśava, the crowned one (Arjuna), trembling, folded his hands and bowed; and bowing again, terrified, he spoke to Kṛṣṇa in a faltering voice.',
 [('etat śrutvā vacanam keśavasya','<b>hearing this word of Keśava</b>'),('kṛta-añjaliḥ vepamānaḥ kirīṭī','<b>with folded hands, trembling, the crowned one</b>'),('namaskṛtvā bhūyaḥ eva āha','<b>bowing again, he said</b>'),('sa-gadgadam bhīta-bhītaḥ praṇamya','<b>in a faltering voice, terrified, bowing</b>')],
 'Sañjaya pauses to describe Arjuna’s state to the blind king.',
 'Commentators note the subtle message to Dhṛtarāṣṭra: your sons are doomed. Sañjaya still hopes the king will stop the war. <i>Kirīṭin</i>, the crowned one, is Arjuna’s name from a crown given by Indra.',
 'Some news is meant to wake us before it is too late.',
 'Arjuna, <b>trembling, bows again</b>.'),
X(36,'THE WORLD DELIGHTS IN YOU',
 CS('The world rejoicing in praise, demons fleeing, sages bowing','HṚṢĪKEŚA'),
 'Arjuna said: Rightly, Hṛṣīkeśa, does the world rejoice and delight in your praise. Demons flee in fear in all directions, and the hosts of the perfected bow to you.',
 [('sthāne hṛṣīkeśa tava prakīrtyā','<b>rightly, Hṛṣīkeśa, in your praise</b>'),('jagat prahṛṣyati anurajyate ca','<b>the world rejoices and delights</b>'),('rakṣāṃsi bhītāni diśaḥ dravanti','<b>demons flee in fear in all directions</b>'),('sarve namasyanti ca siddha-saṅghāḥ','<b>and all the perfected bow</b>')],
 'Arjuna begins a great hymn of praise.',
 'This verse begins one of the most beautiful hymns in the Gītā (11.36–44), often chanted on its own. The verse is used as a protective prayer: demons flee at God’s praise.',
 'Praise lifts the heart and drives away fear.',
 'The world <b>rejoices in your praise</b>.'),
X(37,'WHY SHOULD THEY NOT BOW?',
 CS('Brahma and all beings bowing to the infinite form','ANANTA'),
 '“And why should they not bow to you, great soul, greater even than Brahmā, the first creator? Infinite One, Lord of gods, home of the universe, you are the Imperishable, being and non-being, and what is beyond both.”',
 [('kasmāt ca te na nameran mahātman','<b>why should they not bow to you</b>'),('garīyase brahmaṇaḥ api ādi-kartre','<b>greater than Brahmā, the first maker</b>'),('ananta deva-īśa jagat-nivāsa','<b>infinite, Lord of gods, home of the world</b>'),('tvam akṣaram sat asat tat-param yat','<b>you are the Imperishable, being, non-being and beyond</b>')],
 'Arjuna gives reasons for bowing.',
 '<i>Sat asat tat-param</i>: being, non-being, and what is beyond both. Commentators say God includes the manifest and unmanifest and transcends them.',
 'Bowing to what is greater is not weakness; it is truth.',
 'Beyond being <b>and non-being</b>.'),
X(38,'THE FIRST GOD',
 CS('The ancient Person as the resting place of all','ĀDI-DEVA'),
 '“You are the first God, the ancient Person; you are the supreme resting-place of this universe. You are the knower and the known, and the supreme abode. By you the universe is pervaded, O infinite form.”',
 [('tvam ādi-devaḥ puruṣaḥ purāṇaḥ','<b>you are the first God, the ancient Person</b>'),('tvam asya viśvasya param nidhānam','<b>the supreme resting-place of this universe</b>'),('vettā asi vedyam ca param ca dhāma','<b>the knower, the known, the supreme abode</b>'),('tvayā tatam viśvam ananta-rūpa','<b>by you the world is pervaded, infinite form</b>')],
 'Arjuna names the Lord with many titles.',
 '“The knower and the known”: commentators say God is both the subject and object of all knowledge. Many lines here echo the Upaniṣads and later Purāṇic hymns.',
 'The one who knows and the thing known meet in the Divine.',
 'The knower <b>and the known</b>.'),
X(39,'A THOUSAND SALUTATIONS',
 CS('Arjuna bowing a thousand times to the gods within the form','NAMO NAMAḤ'),
 '“You are Vāyu, Yama, Agni, Varuṇa, the moon, Prajāpati and the great-grandsire. Salutations to you a thousand times, and again and again salutations to you!”',
 [('vāyuḥ yamaḥ agniḥ varuṇaḥ śaśāṅkaḥ','<b>wind, Yama, fire, Varuṇa, the moon</b>'),('prajāpatiḥ tvam prapitāmahaḥ ca','<b>Prajāpati and the great-grandsire</b>'),('namaḥ namaḥ te astu sahasra-kṛtvaḥ','<b>salutations, a thousand times</b>'),('punaḥ ca bhūyaḥ api namaḥ namaḥ te','<b>again and again salutations</b>')],
 'Arjuna sees every god in Kṛṣṇa, and bows endlessly.',
 '<i>Prapitāmaha</i>, great-grandfather: Brahmā is the grandfather of beings, and God is the father of Brahmā. Commentators note the repetition of <i>namaḥ</i>: overflowing devotion.',
 'When love overflows, words repeat. That is natural.',
 'Salutations, <b>a thousand times</b>.'),
X(40,'ON ALL SIDES',
 CS('Arjuna bowing in front, behind and on every side','SARVATAḤ'),
 '“Salutations to you in front and behind; salutations on every side, O All! Infinite in power, immeasurable in strength, you pervade all, and so you are all.”',
 [('namaḥ purastāt atha pṛṣṭhataḥ te','<b>salutations in front and behind</b>'),('namaḥ astu te sarvataḥ eva sarva','<b>salutations on every side, O All</b>'),('ananta-vīrya amita-vikramaḥ tvam','<b>infinite power, boundless might</b>'),('sarvam samāpnoṣi tataḥ asi sarvaḥ','<b>you pervade all, so you are all</b>')],
 'Bowing in every direction, because God is everywhere.',
 '“You pervade all, so you are all”: commentators of Advaita read this literally; Rāmānuja as “all is your body”. The devotion is the same.',
 'Wherever you turn, the Divine is there.',
 'You pervade all, <b>you are all</b>.'),
X(41,'“O KṚṢṆA, O FRIEND”',
 S('Arjuna remembering joking with Krishna as a friend, now ashamed','Af','family','HE SAKHE!'),
 '“Thinking of you as a friend, whatever I said rashly, ‘O Kṛṣṇa, O Yādava, O friend’, not knowing this greatness of yours, through carelessness or affection,”',
 [('sakhā iti matvā prasabham yat uktam','<b>thinking you a friend, whatever I said rashly</b>'),('he kṛṣṇa he yādava he sakhe iti','<b>“O Kṛṣṇa, O Yādava, O friend”</b>'),('ajānatā mahimānam tava idam','<b>not knowing this greatness of yours</b>'),('mayā pramādāt praṇayena vā api','<b>through carelessness or affection</b>')],
 'Arjuna remembers how casually he treated his friend.',
 'A deeply human moment. Arjuna had joked with Kṛṣṇa, ordered him around, called him by name. Now he sees who Kṛṣṇa is. Commentators note the beauty of friendship with God (sakhya bhāva), one of the forms of devotion.',
 'We often take our dearest people for granted. Seeing their worth is a wake-up call.',
 '“O Kṛṣṇa, <b>O friend</b>.”'),
X(42,'FORGIVE ME',
 S('Arjuna begging forgiveness for jokes made at meals and rest','Af','heart','KṢAMAYE'),
 '“…and whatever disrespect I showed you in jest, while playing, resting, sitting or eating, alone or in company, Acyuta: for that I ask forgiveness of you, the Immeasurable.”',
 [('yat ca avahāsa-artham asat-kṛtaḥ asi','<b>whatever disrespect, in jest</b>'),('vihāra-śayyā-āsana-bhojaneṣu','<b>while playing, resting, sitting, eating</b>'),('ekaḥ athavā api acyuta tat-samakṣam','<b>alone, or before others, Acyuta</b>'),('tat kṣāmaye tvām aham aprameyam','<b>for that I ask your forgiveness</b>')],
 'Arjuna asks forgiveness for every casual moment.',
 'The list is touching: playing, lying down, sitting, eating, the ordinary moments of friendship. Commentators note God does not need the apology; Arjuna needs to offer it.',
 'Apologising to those we have taken for granted heals both sides.',
 '<b>Forgive me</b>, Acyuta.'),
X(43,'FATHER OF THE WORLD',
 CS('The form as the father and teacher of all, unmatched','PITĀ · GURU'),
 '“You are the father of this world of moving and unmoving things; you are its worshipful, greatest teacher. There is none equal to you in the three worlds; how could there be any greater, O One of matchless power?”',
 [('pitā asi lokasya cara-acarasya','<b>you are the father of the world</b>'),('tvam asya pūjyaḥ ca guruḥ garīyān','<b>its worshipful and greatest teacher</b>'),('na tvat-samaḥ asti abhyadhikaḥ kutaḥ anyaḥ','<b>none equal, how any greater?</b>'),('loka-traye api apratima-prabhāva','<b>in the three worlds, of matchless power</b>')],
 'God as father and guru of all.',
 'Commentators note the two relationships: father (who gives life) and guru (who gives wisdom). Arjuna now sees Kṛṣṇa as both.',
 'Honour those who gave you life and wisdom. They reflect the Divine.',
 'Father <b>and teacher of all</b>.'),
X(44,'AS A FATHER WITH A SON',
 S('Arjuna prostrate, Krishna gently lifting him like a father','KA','heart','BEAR WITH ME'),
 '“So I bow down, prostrating my body, and seek your grace, adorable Lord. As a father with a son, a friend with a friend, a lover with the beloved, so please bear with me, O God.”',
 [('tasmāt praṇamya praṇidhāya kāyam','<b>so bowing, laying down my body</b>'),('prasādaye tvām aham īśam īḍyam','<b>I seek your grace, adorable Lord</b>'),('pitā iva putrasya sakhā iva sakhyuḥ','<b>as father to son, friend to friend</b>'),('priyaḥ priyāyāḥ arhasi deva soḍhum','<b>lover to beloved, please bear with me</b>')],
 'Three images of loving patience: father, friend, lover.',
 'These three relationships later become three forms of devotion in bhakti traditions: parental, friendly and loving. Commentators find this verse among the tenderest in the Gītā.',
 'Love forgives. Ask for it, and give it.',
 'Bear with me <b>as a father, friend, lover</b>.'),
X(45,'SHOW ME YOUR GENTLE FORM',
 S('Arjuna asking to see Krishna’s familiar form again','Af','vishnu','PRASĪDA'),
 '“I am thrilled to have seen what was never seen before, yet my mind is shaken with fear. Show me, O God, that other form of yours. Be gracious, Lord of gods, home of the universe.”',
 [('adṛṣṭa-pūrvam hṛṣitaḥ asmi dṛṣṭvā','<b>having seen the never-seen, I am thrilled</b>'),('bhayena ca pravyathitam manaḥ me','<b>yet my mind is shaken with fear</b>'),('tat eva me darśaya deva rūpam','<b>show me that form, O God</b>'),('prasīda deva-īśa jagat-nivāsa','<b>be gracious, Lord of gods</b>')],
 'Joy and fear together. Arjuna asks to see the gentle form again.',
 'An honest mixture: thrilled and terrified. Commentators note Arjuna prefers the intimate, loving form to the overwhelming cosmic one, as most devotees do.',
 'The infinite inspires awe; the personal inspires love. Both have their place.',
 'Thrilled, afraid: <b>show me your gentle form</b>.'),
X(46,'THE FOUR-ARMED FORM',
 S('Krishna with four arms, crown, mace and discus','','vishnu','CATUR-BHUJA'),
 '“I wish to see you as before, wearing a crown, holding a mace, discus in hand. Take that four-armed form, thousand-armed one of universal form.”',
 [('kirīṭinam gadinam cakra-hastam','<b>with crown, mace, discus in hand</b>'),('icchāmi tvām draṣṭum aham tathā eva','<b>I wish to see you just so</b>'),('tena eva rūpeṇa catur-bhujena','<b>in that four-armed form</b>'),('sahasra-bāho bhava viśva-mūrte','<b>become it, thousand-armed universal form</b>')],
 'Arjuna asks for the four-armed Viṣṇu form.',
 'Commentators debate: had Arjuna seen this four-armed form before? Some say Kṛṣṇa’s divine form is four-armed; others that Arjuna asks for an intermediate form. Either way, he asks for a form he can approach.',
 'We need forms of the Divine we can relate to. That is natural and good.',
 'Crown, mace, discus: <b>four arms</b>.'),
X(47,'NO ONE BUT YOU HAS SEEN IT',
 S('Krishna explaining that this vision was a rare grace','K','light','PRASANNENA'),
 'The Lord said: Out of grace, Arjuna, by my own power, I have shown you this supreme form, radiant, universal, infinite, primal, which no one but you has seen before.',
 [('mayā prasannena tava arjuna idam','<b>by me, gracious, to you, Arjuna, this</b>'),('rūpam param darśitam ātma-yogāt','<b>supreme form shown by my own power</b>'),('tejomayam viśvam anantam ādyam','<b>radiant, universal, infinite, primal</b>'),('yat me tvat-anyena na dṛṣṭa-pūrvam','<b>which none but you has seen before</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa stresses the rarity of the gift.',
 'Commentators note some earlier figures in the epic saw partial cosmic forms (e.g., Duryodhana in the Kaurava court), but not this fullness. The point is grace: it was given, not earned.',
 'The greatest gifts come by grace. Receive them with gratitude.',
 'Shown <b>by grace alone</b>.'),
X(48,'NOT BY RITUAL OR STUDY',
 S('Rituals, books, gifts and austerities all failing to reveal the form','','book','NOT BY THESE'),
 '“Not by the Vedas, nor by sacrifices, nor by study, nor by gifts, nor by rituals, nor by fierce austerities can I be seen in this form in the human world by anyone but you, hero of the Kurus.”',
 [('na veda-yajña-adhyayanaiḥ na dānaiḥ','<b>not by Vedas, sacrifices, study, gifts</b>'),('na ca kriyābhiḥ na tapobhiḥ ugraiḥ','<b>nor by rites, nor fierce austerities</b>'),('evam-rūpaḥ śakyaḥ aham nṛ-loke','<b>can I be seen in this form in the human world</b>'),('draṣṭum tvat-anyena kuru-pravīra','<b>by anyone other than you</b>')],
 'This vision cannot be earned by any practice.',
 'Commentators: practices purify, but this vision is a gift. The next verses say what does open it: devotion.',
 'Effort prepares; grace reveals.',
 'Not by effort <b>alone</b>.'),
X(49,'DON’T BE AFRAID',
 S('Krishna comforting a shaken Arjuna','KA','heart','MĀ TE VYATHĀ'),
 '“Do not be afraid or bewildered at seeing this terrible form of mine. Free from fear and glad at heart, see again that form of mine.”',
 [('mā te vyathā mā ca vimūḍha-bhāvaḥ','<b>do not fear, do not be bewildered</b>'),('dṛṣṭvā rūpam ghoram īdṛk mama idam','<b>seeing this terrible form of mine</b>'),('vyapeta-bhīḥ prīta-manāḥ punaḥ tvam','<b>fear gone, glad at heart, again you</b>'),('tat eva me rūpam idam prapaśya','<b>behold that form of mine</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa comforts Arjuna.',
 'After the overwhelming vision, gentle words: <i>mā te vyathā</i>, “don’t be afraid”. Commentators note the Lord’s tenderness balances his majesty.',
 'After great shocks, gentle reassurance heals. Give it to others.',
 '<b>Don’t be afraid</b>.'),
X(50,'THE GENTLE FORM RETURNS',
 S('Krishna returning to his gentle human form, Arjuna calmer','KA','light','SAUMYA-VAPUḤ'),
 'Sañjaya said: Having spoken thus to Arjuna, Vāsudeva showed his own form again; and the great soul, taking his gentle form once more, comforted the terrified Arjuna.',
 [('iti arjunam vāsudevaḥ tathā uktvā','<b>thus speaking to Arjuna, Vāsudeva</b>'),('svakam rūpam darśayām āsa bhūyaḥ','<b>showed his own form again</b>'),('āśvāsayām āsa ca bhītam enam','<b>and comforted the frightened one</b>'),('bhūtvā punaḥ saumya-vapuḥ mahātmā','<b>becoming gentle-bodied again, the great soul</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa becomes the gentle friend again.',
 '“His own form”: commentators debate whether this is the four-armed or the two-armed human form; many say first four-armed, then two-armed. <i>Saumya</i>, gentle, moon-like.',
 'The Divine comes close in gentle forms. That is its kindness.',
 'Gentle <b>once more</b>.'),
X(51,'I AM MYSELF AGAIN',
 S('Arjuna calm again, looking at Krishna’s human form','KA','heart','PRAKṚTIṂ GATAḤ'),
 'Arjuna said: Seeing this gentle human form of yours, Janārdana, I am now composed, and restored to my normal self.',
 [('dṛṣṭvā idam mānuṣam rūpam','<b>seeing this human form</b>'),('tava saumyam janārdana','<b>your gentle one, Janārdana</b>'),('idānīm asmi saṃvṛttaḥ','<b>now I am composed</b>'),('sa-cetāḥ prakṛtim gataḥ','<b>with my mind, restored to my nature</b>')],
 'Arjuna is himself again.',
 'Commentators note Arjuna is not the same as before: he has seen, and that will stay with him. But he can now function. Vision must return to daily life.',
 'After a great experience, return to ordinary life changed but steady.',
 'Composed, <b>myself again</b>.'),
X(52,'EVEN THE GODS LONG TO SEE IT',
 S('Gods looking down longing to see what Arjuna saw','','gods','EVEN THE GODS'),
 'The Lord said: This form of mine that you have seen is very hard to see. Even the gods are always longing for a sight of it.',
 [('su-durdarśam idam rūpam','<b>very hard to see is this form</b>'),('dṛṣṭavān asi yat mama','<b>which you have seen, of mine</b>'),('devāḥ api asya rūpasya','<b>even the gods, of this form</b>'),('nityam darśana-kāṅkṣiṇaḥ','<b>always long for a sight</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa underlines Arjuna’s good fortune.',
 'Which form is “hard to see”: the cosmic form, or the human form now before him? Commentators of devotional schools say even the human form, rightly seen, is the rarest sight.',
 'What is in front of us may be more precious than we know.',
 'Even the gods <b>long to see it</b>.'),
X(53,'NOT BY VEDAS OR GIFTS',
 S('Books, gifts and rituals set aside','','book','NOT BY THESE'),
 '“Not by the Vedas, nor by austerity, nor by gifts, nor by sacrifice can I be seen in the form you have seen me.”',
 [('na aham vedaiḥ na tapasā','<b>not by Vedas, nor austerity</b>'),('na dānena na ca ijyayā','<b>nor gifts, nor sacrifice</b>'),('śakyaḥ evam-vidhaḥ draṣṭum','<b>can I be seen in this way</b>'),('dṛṣṭavān asi mām yathā','<b>as you have seen me</b>')],
 'A repetition of 11.48 for emphasis.',
 'Repeated so that it sinks in: no practice alone wins this vision. The next verse gives the key.',
 'Repetition marks what matters most.',
 'Not by practice <b>alone</b>.'),
X(54,'ONLY BY DEVOTION',
 S('A devotee with undivided love, a door opening to Krishna','KA','heart','ANANYA BHAKTI'),
 '“But by undivided devotion, Arjuna, I can be known in this way, seen in truth, and entered, scorcher of foes.”',
 [('bhaktyā tu ananyayā śakyaḥ','<b>but by undivided devotion, possible</b>'),('aham evam-vidhaḥ arjuna','<b>am I in this way</b>'),('jñātum draṣṭum ca tattvena','<b>to know, to see in truth</b>'),('praveṣṭum ca parantapa','<b>and to enter</b>')],
 'The key: undivided devotion.',
 'Three stages: knowing, seeing, entering. Commentators of all schools agree devotion is decisive here; they differ on how devotion and knowledge relate. Rāmānuja sees this verse as central.',
 'Love opens doors that effort cannot.',
 'Only by <b>undivided devotion</b>.'),
X(55,'THE ESSENCE OF THE GĪTĀ',
 S('A devotee working for God, free of attachment and hatred','KA','heart','MAT-KARMA-KṚT'),
 '“One who works for me, has me as the supreme goal, is devoted to me, free from attachment, without enmity toward any being: he comes to me, Pāṇḍava.”',
 [('mat-karma-kṛt mat-paramaḥ','<b>working for me, me as the goal</b>'),('mat-bhaktaḥ saṅga-varjitaḥ','<b>devoted to me, free of attachment</b>'),('nirvairaḥ sarva-bhūteṣu','<b>without enmity to any being</b>'),('yaḥ saḥ mām eti pāṇḍava','<b>he comes to me</b>')],
 'Śaṅkara called this verse the essence of the whole Gītā.',
 'Five marks: work for God, God as goal, devotion, non-attachment, and no hatred toward anyone. Śaṅkara’s commentary calls it the sum of the Gītā’s teaching. Note the last: even after a battlefield vision, the ideal is <i>nirvaira</i>, without enmity.',
 'Work for the highest, love, let go, and hate no one. That is the whole path.',
 'Without enmity <b>toward any being</b>.'),
]
